Fluoroquinolone-Induced Tendinopathy and Tendon Rupture.

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient R.B.
* Age: 68 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 76 kg
* Medical History: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD), mild osteoarthritis, no previous history of tendonitis or Achilles tendon injury.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Diagnosed with an acute bacterial exacerbation of COPD; prescribed Levofloxacin 500 mg orally once daily for 10 days.
* Month 1 (Day 8): Patient developed sudden, severe bilateral posterior ankle pain, localized swelling, and stiffness upon weight-bearing.
* Month 1 (Day 10): Experienced a sudden popping sound in the right heel while walking, resulting in immediate inability to plantarfles or bear weight on the right leg.
* Diagnostic and Clinical Findings at Admission:
  * Physical Exam: Positive Thompson squeeze test on the right leg (absent plantar flexion); visible gap palpable 4 cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion.
  * Ankle Ultrasound: Complete full-thickness tear of the right Achilles tendon and high-grade tendinopathy of the left Achilles tendon.
  * Systemic Markers: Normal inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP within normal reference ranges).
* Intervention: Immediate permanent cessation of Levofloxacin; patient referred to orthopedic surgery for right Achilles tendon surgical repair and left ankle immobilization.
* Follow-up (Month 4 post-discontinuation): Completed physical therapy; right Achilles tendon healed post-repair, left tendinopathy fully resolved without secondary rupture.

